@*
    You.

    WHAT THE PHONE KNOWS ABOUT YOU, gathered once and used everywhere. The CV
    interview already asks for a name, a number, where somebody lives and what they
    do - and then buries it in a career database only the CV screen can reach. A
    letter, an invoice, a clinic form and an application to a landlord all want the
    same facts, and today each would ask again.

    So the profile is the thing and the CV is one document made FROM it.

    It is on the bar because this is the most personal thing the app holds. Being
    able to see it, change it, and delete it is the other half of promising it
    never leaves the phone.

    NOTHING SCROLLS: five fields and two buttons, whole on a 344px screen.
*@
@page "/you"
@inject IProfile Store
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<div class="you">

    <div class="bar-line">
        <span class="done">@($"{_profile.Completeness * 100:0}%") complete</span>
        <span class="track"><span style="width:@($"{_profile.Completeness * 100:0}%")"></span></span>
    </div>

    @foreach (var fact in _profile.Facts)
    {
        <label class="field" @key="fact.Key">
            <span class="field-label">@fact.Label</span>
            @if (fact.Multiline)
            {
                <textarea class="field-input" rows="2" placeholder="@fact.Hint"
                          value="@fact.Value"
                          @onchange="e => Save(fact.Key, e)"></textarea>
            }
            else
            {
                <input class="field-input" placeholder="@fact.Hint"
                       value="@fact.Value"
                       @onchange="e => Save(fact.Key, e)" />
            }
        </label>
    }

    <div class="actions">
        <button class="soft" @onclick='() => Nav.NavigateTo("career")'>Make my CV</button>
        <button class="danger" @onclick="Forget">Forget me</button>
    </div>

    @if (_said is not null)
    {
        <p class="said">@_said</p>
    }

</div>

@code {
    Profile _profile = new([], 0);
    string? _said;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync() => _profile = await Store.LoadAsync();

    /// <summary>
    /// Saved on leaving the field, not on every keystroke.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A write per character would hit SQLite on every letter of somebody's name.
    /// @onchange fires when the field loses focus, which is also when a person has
    /// finished the thought - and there is no save button, so what is on screen is
    /// always what is stored.
    /// </remarks>
    async Task Save(string key, ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        await Store.SetAsync(key, e.Value?.ToString() ?? "");
        _profile = await Store.LoadAsync();
    }

    async Task Forget()
    {
        await Store.ForgetAsync();
        _profile = await Store.LoadAsync();
        _said = "Everything it knew about you is gone from this phone.";
    }
}
